#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b7c316a-2ec9-5f4c-853c-e82b09755db0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93a524bc-dbe0-527a-9025-3d60ee66662c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369114} {:answer/id #uuid "4f701a29-414c-5242-af93-500e7082a757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369115} {:answer/id #uuid "2263fea8-99ab-5528-9114-c1a5f4d13e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369116} {:answer/id #uuid "dfbfd963-2906-5722-b152-e3c6726d4626" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369117} {:answer/id #uuid "4e3d0790-793b-5370-982b-173ea365929b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2263fea8-99ab-5528-9114-c1a5f4d13e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369116} :answer-field/id #uuid "210210bc-cd82-5454-b1dc-84e092e994aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6d64847-1769-5602-97fa-762ad6861828" :question/math-academy-id "q-338142" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+5y=4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constant omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,dx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)^{′}-({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{′}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2{e}^{2x}{cos}^{2}⁡2x-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2{e}^{2x}{sin}^{2}⁡2x \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}({cos}^{2}⁡2x+{sin}^{2}⁡2x) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x\\cdot {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-2\\int dx \\\\  & =-2x \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x\\cdot {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}+{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b7c316a-2ec9-5f4c-853c-e82b09755db0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94d95791-2cc7-5a8b-962f-73c236a5c448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369121} {:answer/id #uuid "de4cbfee-dc26-5f53-9de8-a54d1fed0a56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369122} {:answer/id #uuid "bc64cc80-c370-594d-8ddb-00cdb5c8bcb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369123} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbc4f3e-ef1a-51f8-837a-6b1ed0325ebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369124} {:answer/id #uuid "c11e43fe-ba7a-53c0-90d7-a59aa3b96b03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bbc4f3e-ef1a-51f8-837a-6b1ed0325ebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369124} :answer-field/id #uuid "01fdd2a3-162b-5f4d-bd71-bfbd4cb30ce6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cb7b8734-1edc-5c36-87f9-ef5befb2dd32" :question/math-academy-id "q-338767" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+5y=x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)={e}^{2x}\\,(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following results (integration constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=x{e}^{2x}.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={e}^{2x}\\,(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{x{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{e}^{4x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{x{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{e}^{4x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\,(x{cos}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x{sin}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =x{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\,(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{{x{e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=x{e}^{2x}.\n$$"}]}
